nThe pack's filed wtd-avg share series (FY2025: 134.7M) is on ORDINARY-SHARE basis;\nvendor market data (33.56M) is on ADS basis. Ratio = 4.02, consistent with the\n1-ADS:4-ordinary-shares structure. Cross-check: FY2025 GAAP net income $123.0M\n[Actual] / 33.56M ADS = $3.66/ADS, matching WallStreetZen \"current EPS $3.64\"\n[Aggregator]. All per-share values below are per ADS. The pack's vendor beta (1.714)\nis used only as an input to the discount-rate sensitivity, not blindly.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 0. ENTERPRISE CLASSIFICATION & ENGINE SELECTION\nArchetype: C (Cyclical) at early transition to D/E (Product-Platform Hybrid /\nOption-Led). Evidence:\n  1. 12-year filed history shows classic cyclicality: Rev $0.556B (2016) \u2192 $0.457B\n     (2019) \u2192 $0.922B (2021) \u2192 $0.639B (2023) \u2192 $0.886B (2025) [Actual \u2014 SEC filings].\n  2. Q2 2026 marks an AI-inflected inflection: Rev $451M (+127% YoY), Ferri/enterprise\n     boot-drive mix from 4% to 30% of revenue within 4 quarters [Actual \u2014 company\n     statements via Investing.com transcript 2026-07-30].\n  3. MonTitan enterprise controller began shipping Q2 2026, targeting 5-10% of revenue\n     by Q4 2026 [Actual \u2014 company statement] \u2014 option-like ramp, not yet evidence of a\n     durable platform.\n\nTransition criteria (Cyclical \u2192 Quality/Hybrid): ROIC>WACC for 3 cycles \u2014 NOT met;\nmargin volatility reduced \u2014 NOT YET met (GM range 48.3-50.2% is improving but only\n2 quarters of data). Assessment: ~40-50% of transition criteria met \u2192 Blended Engine\nper Step 0-3.\n\nEngine selection: ENGINE 2 (Cycle-Adjusted) \u00d7 50% + ENGINE 4 (Expectation-Driven) \u00d7 50%.\nUnderlying mechanics use Engine 1 DCF discipline with explicit forecasts.\nWhy not a pure Engine? A pure cycle engine undervalues the structural AI-storage mix\nshift; a pure option engine overvalues unproven optionality at the current price.\nErrors if a pure engine were used: pure Engine 4 would justify the price on narrative\nalone (forbidden by Output Rule 3); pure Engine 2 would ignore a real, order-backed\nmix change.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n1-1. Macro variables [Actual]:\n  \u2022 10Y UST: 4.71% (TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21) \u2014 up 0.44pts YoY; real yield ~2%+.\n  \u2022 Fed: July 2026 MPR \u2014 inflation \"remains above the 2% objective\"; policy neutral-to-\n    restrictive under Chair Warsh (IronFX Fed guide, 2026-07; Bloomberg, 2026-08-24).\n  \u2022 DXY: strong-to-stable; \"debasement trade\" cited in bond commentary (Investing.com).\n  \u2022 Credit spreads: contained; equity leverage norms not stressed.\n  \u2022 NAND industry: shortage through 2026; TrendForce sees normalization in 2027\n    [Estimate \u2014 TechPowerUp/TrendForce]; management claims shortage may persist to\n    2028 [Actual \u2014 company statement]. Conflict unresolved \u2192 scenario input.\n\n1-2. Regime probabilities: Goldilocks 30% / Reflation 40% / Stagflation 15% /\nRecession 15% (sum 100%). Base is Reflation: high-but-stable rates + real AI-driven\ngrowth; tail risk is stagflationary (rates high, AI capex digestion begins).\n\n1-3. Sensitivity (each -3 to +3): Rates -2 (long-duration FCF, high beta); Dollar -1\n(A'sia revenue, US-listed); Liquidity +2 (AI capex supercycle); Cycle +1 (memory\ncycle up); Commodity +1 (NAND pricing tailwind).\n\n1-4. Macro alignment: Regime-weighted impact = slightly negative (rates dominate).\nJudgment: NEUTRAL-to-HEADWIND. No conviction add; do not initiate at a valuation\nthat assumes low discount rates.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 1.5. BASE RATES & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n  \u2022 Cyclical semi revenue spikes reverting \u226540% within 3 years: base rate ~30-40%\n    [Framework reference]. SIMO has done exactly this twice in the pack window\n    (2016-2019: -18%; 2021-2023: -31%).\n  \u2022 Platform-transition success (cyclical \u2192 structural compounder): base rate\n    ~20-30% [Framework reference].\n  \u2022 This analysis assigns Bull 20% \u2014 at the upper edge of the transition base rate.\n    Justification for the deviation (3 items): (1) MonTitan design-win pipeline with\n    flash makers and cloud service providers is contract-based, not narrative\n    [Actual \u2014 company statement]; (2) boot-drive attach of ~40 drives/AI server rack\n    is a concrete unit driver [Actual \u2014 CEO on Q2 call via Fool]; (3) existing\n    NAND-maker relationships give supply allocation during shortages [Actual \u2014 CEO].\n  \u2022 Base-rate-shock check: assigned Bull is 1.0x the transition base rate \u2014 no red\n    flag. However, market price implies a Bull probability >50% (see Layer 4.5) \u2014\n    that IS a red flag against the base rate and requires heavy evidence to accept.\n    That evidence does not exist in the pack or public record. \u2192 The market price\n    itself fails the base-rate shock check.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n2-1. What it does: Fabless designer of NAND flash controllers (SSD, eMMC/UFS, and\nincreasingly enterprise/AI boot drives and MonTitan enterprise controllers). Sells\nto NAND makers and module makers; monetizes per-controller + royalty. Understandability:\nHigh.\n\n2-2. Moat score: Intangibles (IP) 2 | Switching costs (qualification cycles) 2 |\nNetwork effects 0 | Cost advantage 1 | Scale 2 | Regulatory 0 | Data 1 \u2192 TOTAL 8/21\n\u2192 NARROW moat. Direction: consumer/mobile \u2014 STABLE-to-NARROWING (Phison, Maxio,\nNAND-maker in-sourcing); enterprise/AI (MonTitan, boot drives) \u2014 WIDENING (early share,\nNAND allocation). Net direction: STABLE.\n\n2-3. Lynch classification: CYCLICAL, with a fast-growing segment. Traditional\ncheckpoint: at cyclical peaks, P/E is lowest and danger is highest \u2014 current stock\nprice embeds peak-cycle earnings power (see Layer 3).\n\n2-4. Financial strength: FY25 GM 48.3%, OI margin 10.5% (median 19.0%), FY25 FCF\n$6M, net cash \u2248$199M [Actual \u2014 pack]. Balance sheet: underlevered. Scorecard:\nADEQUATE-to-GOOD. Note FCF conversion at peak growth is the weak point (OCF $61M\nvs NI $123M in FY25).\n\n2-5. Capital allocation (0-15): Buyback efficiency 1 (no meaningful repurchase vs\nSBC), M&A 2, Capex efficiency 2 (incremental ROIC unproven), Mix rationality 2,\nGuidance accuracy 3 (beat Q2 rev by 12%, EPS by 15%) \u2192 TOTAL 10/15. Insider bonus:\n0 (insider ownership 3.37%, no net-buy signal). Score 10/15 = GOOD.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n  \u2022 R&D capitalization: FY25 implied R&D \u2248 $298M (derived: OI $93M - GM $428M;\n    SG&A per pack is G&A-only at $37M, so R&D \u2248 $298M) [Estimate]. Capitalizing\n    3-year amortization \u2192 adjusted OI uplift \u2248 +$5M only (run-rate R&D \u2248 amortization).\n  \u2022 SBC: $26M in FY25 [Actual \u2014 pack] treated as full expense (already in opex).\n  \u2022 Adjusted ROIC: FY25 NOPAT \u2248 $105M; invested capital = equity $831M + LT debt $3M\n    - cash $202M \u2248 $632M \u2192 unadjusted ROIC \u2248 17%; capitalized-R&D IC \u2248 $1.5B \u2192\n    adjusted ROIC \u2248 12-14%. Above WACC, but NOT a fortress.\n  \u2022 Owner earnings FY25: NI $123M + D&A $30M - maintenance capex (~$40M est.)\n    - \u0394WC $8M \u2248 $105M [Estimate]. Conversion is poor.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 3. VALUATION ENGINE (BLENDED E2 \u00d7 50% + E4 \u00d7 50%)\n\n3-1. Discount rate derivation:\n  \u2022 Rf (10Y UST): 4.71% [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics 2026-08-21]\n  \u2022 ERP: 4.75% [Assumption]\n  \u2022 Beta: 1.6 [Assumption \u2014 blend of vendor 1.714 [Aggregator] and fundamental\n    cyclical-beta ~1.5; the vendor window is unknown]\n  \u2022 CoE = 4.71% + 1.6 \u00d7 4.75% = 12.31%\n  \u2022 Debt: $3M LT debt + ~$14M operating leases [Actual \u2014 pack]; weight \u2192 ~0.2%;\n    after-tax cost \u2248 0%. WACC \u2248 12.3% [Assumption].\n  Sensitivity noted: at 10.5% WACC, base IV rises ~40%; at 9.5%, base IV ~+60%.\n  Using 12.3% is a policy choice for a cyclical with \u03b2~1.6.\n\n3-2. Forecast path (source every driver):\n  FY2026 revenue $1,850M [Estimate \u2014 anchored on: company statement FY26 \"more than\n  double\" 2025 [Actual]; Q1 $342M (derived), Q2 $451M [Actual], Q3 guide midpoint\n  $530M [Actual]; Q4 assumed $527M (+4% QoQ) [Assumption]].\n  FY2027 $2,150M (+16%) [Assumption \u2014 TrendForce normalization offsets MonTitan ramp;\n  above BofA's $2B+ 2028 view [Estimate \u2014 TipRanks]].\n  FY2028 $2,400M [Assumption]; FY2029 $2,650M [Assumption]; FY2030 $2,900M\n  [Assumption]. Post-2026 growth is NOT established by any company guidance \u2014 the\n  path shown is my assumption and the scenarios bracket it.\n  GM%: 50.2% FY26 [Estimate \u2014 Q2 50.2%, Q3 guide 50-51%], fading to 48.0% by FY2030\n  [Assumption \u2014 shortage normalization, competitive response].\n  EBIT%: 22.5% FY26 [Assumption \u2014 Q1 ~19%, Q2 23.1% [Actual], Q3 guide 27.5-28.5%\n  [Actual], exit >30% [Actual]; blended FY], 23.5% FY27, 24.5% FY28-30 [Assumption].\n  Tax 15% [Assumption \u2014 Taiwan incentive rate historical; not separately disclosed].\n  D&A = 2.5% of revenue [Assumption \u2014 FY25 3.4%, declining trend].\n  Capex = 6% of revenue [Assumption \u2014 FY25 6.2%, rising trend].\n  \u0394WC = 30% of incremental revenue [Assumption \u2014 Tier 4; historical range 19-45%,\n  recent 3-yr avg ~42%; 30% is a moderate normalization. Q1 2026 showed \"heavy\n  working capital drain\" [Estimate \u2014 web research brief]].\n\n  DCF TABLE ($M):\n  FY        Rev    EBIT%  EBIT   NOPAT  D&A   Capex  \u0394WC   FCFF\n  2026E     1,850  22.5%  416    354    46    111    289   0\n  2027E     2,150  23.5%  505    429    54    129    90    264\n  2028E     2,400  24.5%  588    500    60    144    75    341\n  2029E     2,650  24.5%  649    552    66    159    75    384\n  2030E     2,900  24.5%  711    604    73    174    75    428\n  Terminal FCFF = 604 \u00d7 (1 - g/ROIC) with g=3% [Assumption], ROIC 20% \u2192 513.\n  Terminal value = 513 / (0.123 - 0.03) = $5,516M.\n  PV(FCFF 26-30) = 0 + 209 + 241 + 242 + 240 = $932M.\n  PV(TV) = 5,516 \u00d7 0.560 = $3,089M.\n  EV = $4,021M. + Net cash ($202M cash + $0 STI - $3M debt) \u2248 +$199M \u2192 Equity value\n  \u2248 $4,220M \u2192 BASE IV = $126/ADS.\n\n3-3. Reverse DCF (market expectation check): Price $258 \u2192 equity $8.66B \u2192 EV \u2248 $8.47B.\n  At WACC 12.3%, g 3%: implied perpetual FCFF cash flow = 8.47 \u00d7 0.093 = $788M \u2192\n  NOPAT \u2248 $1.05B \u2192 EBIT \u2248 $1.24B \u2192 revenue needed by 2030 at 24.5% EBIT margin:\n  \u2248 $5.0B. The market is pricing ~$5B FY2030 revenue (5.6x FY26E). At a market-\n  preferred 10.5% WACC: implied revenue ~$3.7-4.0B. The current price embeds a\n  Top-decile AI-winner outcome and a lower discount rate than a cyclical deserves.\n\n3-4. Multiple cross-check: FY26E P/E \u2248 258/10.5 \u2248 25x; EV/Sales \u2248 4.6x; PEG (vendor-\n  computed on one-year growth) 0.7 is not meaningful at a cyclical peak \u2014 the\n  \"G\" will not persist. Historical mid-cycle P/E for this company has been 12-20x\n  [Estimate \u2014 12-yr pack range]. At 20x FY27E EPS ($11.7 adjusted [Assumption]),\n  value \u2248 $234 \u2014 still below price.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n  \u2022 Competitive response: If MonTitan scales, expect Phison/Marvell price competition\n    and renewed NAND-maker in-sourcing (already seen 2015-2020). Impact: -2.\n  \u2022 Reflexivity: High stock price \u2192 cheap equity for talent (SBC is modest, $26M/yr)\n    and customer trust during shortage. Low stock price risk muted (net cash).\n  \u2022 Customer evolution: early-adopter AI/cloud \u2192 mainstream \u2192 late. The boot-drive\n    niche (~40 drives/rack) is a low-attachment-value socket; NAND makers \"don't\n    bother\" today [Actual \u2014 CEO] \u2014 that statement from the CEO is also the risk.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\nPrior (Layer 1.5): transition success ~25%. Evidence update: Q1-Q2 2026 revenue\nbeats and Q3 guide [Actual] raise likelihood of near-term success; TrendForce 2027\nnormalization [Estimate] and valuation level lower longer-term odds.\nPosterior:\n  \u2022 BULL (20%) \u2014 AI franchise: FY30 Rev $4.3B, EBIT 26%. IV \u2248 $165.\n  \u2022 BASE (45%) \u2014 Strong growth, normalized cycle: DCF above. IV \u2248 $126.\n  \u2022 BEAR (25%) \u2014 NAND normalization hits 2027, price war in enterprise, consumer\n    stays soft: FY30 Rev $2.1B, EBIT 17%. IV \u2248 $60.\n  \u2022 CRISIS (10%) \u2014 AI storage digestion + margin compression: peak-to-trough -20%.\n    IV \u2248 $35.\n\nExpected value = 0.20\u00d7165 + 0.45\u00d7126 + 0.25\u00d760 + 0.10\u00d735 = 33 + 57 + 15 + 4 =\n$109/ADS. 50% CI: $60-$165. 80% CI: $35-$200 (upper tail capped by low-rate bull).\nMargin of Safety = (109 - 258) / 109 = -137% \u2192 MoS: NONE.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 4.5. TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\nMarket horizon: reverse DCF implies 2030 revenue ~$5B \u2014 market looks ~4-5 years out.\nMy horizon: 3 years. Spread < 2 years \u2192 no arbitrage edge. The market is not\nshortsighted; it is optimistically discounting the AI story at a low rate.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\nConviction score (0-15):\n  1. Business quality 2.5 | 2. Valuation attractiveness 0.5 | 3. Macro alignment 1.5\n  4. Thesis clarity 2.5 (KPIs: MonTitan mix, exit OM, mix shift) | 5. Execution\n  traceability 2.5 \u2192 TOTAL 9.5/15 \u2192 MEDIUM.\n  Decay: -1.0 (no confirming evidence for 6 months) \u2192 8.5.\nPosition sizing: Medium conviction = 4-6% guideline, BUT Layer 4 MoS is negative\n(<0%) and Layer 9 gap < 2 \u2192 no new position. Held position: trim to \u22642% [Assumption/\nPolicy].\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n  \u2022 Short interest 2.75% of float, 0.89 days to cover [Aggregator] \u2014 no squeeze\n    dynamics; month-on-month short interest +3.4% (rising).\n  \u2022 Institutional ownership 75.0% [Aggregator] \u2014 highly crowded; ETF flows dominate\n    the marginal share.\n  \u2022 Insider ownership 3.37% [Aggregator] \u2014 low skin in the game; no cluster buying\n    data.\n  \u2022 Options/sentiment: stock -27% from 52-wk high $355 [Aggregator] after +34%\n    one-day spike faded \u2014 classic distribution behavior. Sell-side mean +35% upside\n    [Aggregator] \u2014 high target $450, low $145 (low target \u2248 my bear-case IV).\n  \u2022 Crowding Risk Score: 10/15 (75% institutional, +179% 1-yr return, sell-side\n    enthusiasm, -27% recent drawdown) \u2192 position size reduction required (to \u22642%).\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\nHigh-beta (1.7) cyclical semiconductor with a long-duration AI narrative \u2014 correlation\nto growth/AI basket high; tail correlation to rate shocks high (10Y at 4.71%, rising).\nFor a portfolio already long AI infrastructure: SIMO adds factor concentration, not\ndiversification. If held, treat as part of the AI-risk bucket, not semis-value bucket.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 6.5. KELLY SIZING\nEdge estimate: (Expected value - Price)/Price = (109 - 258)/258 = -58%. Negative\nedge \u2192 Kelly optimal weight = 0%. Any positive weight requires capping at the\nframework's behavioral override (\u22642% for held positions).\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\nCatalysts (12-18 months):\n  1. Q3 2026 report (Oct/Nov 2026): MonTitan >=5-10% of revenue; OM exit >30%\n     [Actual \u2014 company guidance] \u2014 if confirmed, supports bull.\n  2. Q4 2026 / FY27 guidance: need FY27 guide >= $2.1B to support base case.\n  3. NAND shortage duration: CEO says 2028; TrendForce says 2027 [conflict].\nRisks:\n  1. NAND cycle normalization (2027) \u2014 margin and growth reset.\n  2. Enterprise controller price war with Phison/Marvell/NAND-maker in-sourcing.\n  3. Working capital drain \u2014 FY26E FCFF \u2248 $0; inventory cycle turn would amplify\n     cash strain.\n  4. GAAP vs adjusted gap: Q2 GAAP EPS $3.99 vs adjusted $2.43; $54.5M non-recurring\n     item not explained in pack [Unconfirmed \u2014 no reconciliation available].\n  5. Taiwan geopolitical risk (company risk factors, pack).\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\nCommon bear trigger: AI-capex digestion + rate shock. In 2022-type tape (rates up,\nAI de-rating), a 1.7-beta semi with a crowded institutional base, at 25x forward\npeak earnings: CVaR at 5% \u2248 -50% to -65% (to $90-130), consistent with my bear/\ncrisis IV band. Portfolio impact: treat SIMO as a high-beta AI-equity position, not\na defensive cyclical.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 8. MONITORING & THESIS INTEGRITY\nIf held, these KPIs trigger re-evaluation:\n  1. MonTitan quarterly revenue < 5% of revenue by Q4 2026 \u2192 thesis fails.\n  2. FY2026 adjusted EPS < $8.00 \u2192 growth less than doubled \u2192 base case wrong.\n  3. FY27 revenue guide < $1.9B \u2192 cycle normalization earlier than base case.\n  4. OCF conversion < 50% of adjusted NI for two consecutive quarters \u2192 liquidity\n     risk mounting.\n  5. Phison/Marvell enterprise price cuts >10% QoQ \u2192 margin structure breaking.\n  6. GM < 46% in any quarter \u2192 shortage pricing power gone.\nPre-mortem (3 failure scenarios, 5 years out): (a) MonTitan lost enterprise share to\nincumbents \u2014 early sign: design-win announcements stop; (b) NAND makers in-source\nboot controllers \u2014 early sign: customer concentration shifts to 2 firms; (c) The\nAI super-cycle busts \u2014 early sign: hyperscaler capex guides cut, NAND spot prices\nturn down.\nRe-entry zone: $95-$140 (probability-weighted IV band) OR confirmed evidence of\n(a) FY27 guide \u2265 $2.3B, (b) MonTitan at 10%+ of revenue with GM \u2265 52%, (c) positive\nFCF for two consecutive quarters with OCF conversion \u2265 70%.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\nShort thesis (5 bullets):\n  1. SIMO's revenue history is a repeating cycle of spike-and-crash; the current\n     +127% growth is shortage-amplified (NAND supply), and TrendForce sees\n     normalization in 2027.\n  2. Enterprise SSD controllers are contested by Marvell, Phison, InnoGrit and\n     NAND makers' in-house designs \u2014 MonTitan is a late entrant with zero\n     (pre-2026) enterprise pedigree.\n  3. The boot-drive \"40 per rack\" niche is a low-value socket that NAND makers can\n     absorb; the CEO's own rationale (\"volumes too small for NAND makers\") is a\n     rationale that reverses as volumes grow.\n  4. Working capital is consuming all cash (FY26E FCFF \u2248 $0); a demand downturn\n     would force inventory writedowns on top of the revenue decline \u2014 the FY23\n     pattern (Rev -32%, OI -81%) repeats.\n  5. At $258 the stock trades at 25x FY26E EPS that itself assumes a doubling of\n     revenue this year; historically the market has paid 12-20x for this company.\n  6. Q2 GAAP EPS ($3.99) far exceeds adjusted EPS ($2.43) \u2014 a $54.5M unexplained\n     gap [Unconfirmed] that flatters trailing GAAP multiples.\nLogic combat: Long evidence = Q1-Q2 2026 execution, MonTitan tape-outs/design wins,\nNAND allocation access. Short evidence = cyclical base rates, competitive response\nwindows, valuation gap. Score: Long 6/10 vs. Short 7/10 \u2192 gap < 2 \u2192 conviction\ndowngrade required (already applied).\nKilled arguments: (a) \"This time is different \u2014 AI is structural\": counter-evidence\nis the 2021-2023 cycle where the SAME company fell 31% during a structural memory\nboom. Valid only if MonTitan reaches >30% of revenue with proven share gains at\nstable margins. (b) \"NAND shortage to 2028 (CEO)\": overridden by TrendForce's\nindependent 2027 forecast. Valid if supply-side discipline holds at 3 major NAND\nmakers \u2014 tracking Q4 2026 capex announcements.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 10. AUDIT RESULTS (selected; 37-point internal run)\n  \u2022 Methodology: Blended engine justified; DCF not sole basis (Layer 3-4 cross-checks\n    used) \u2714 | Macro included \u2714 | No narrative-only conclusion \u2714\n  \u2022 Valuation: WACC/terminal disclosed; sensitivity to WACC shown (at 9.5%: base\n    IV \u2248 $205 \u2014 still below price) \u2714\n  \u2022 Bias: Overconfidence audit \u2014 BS case bears the burden: Bull (20%) matches\n    transition base rate; the market's implied >50% probability FAILS the base-rate\n    threshold \u2192 red flag recorded \u2714\n  \u2022 Data integrity: Pack limitations honored \u2014 no quarterly/TTM data; FY25 share\n    count unverified; segment data absent; GAAP-to-adjusted Q2 gap [Unconfirmed];\n    consensus EPS conflicting ($5.75 vs $8.33-9.91 across sources) \u2014 scenario range\n    widened accordingly \u2714\n  \u2022 FAIL ITEMS: (i) No analyst Q&A transcript available \u2014 2027+ guidance could not\n    be pinned [Unconfirmed]; (ii) Operating-lease and debt-tag overlap unresolved \u2014\n    but NAV is negligible (<0.5% of market cap), so no materiality; (iii) Tax rate\n    15% is assumption-graded.\n  Net: audit does not change the conclusion; it widens the CI.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\nDate: 2026-08-24 | T0: $258.36 | Base IV: $126 | Prob.-weighted IV: $109\nKey assumptions: FY26E rev $1,850M (+109%); FY30E rev $2,900M; FY30 EBIT% 24.5%;\nD&A 2.5% of rev; capex 6% of rev; \u0394WC 30% of \u0394Rev; WACC 12.3%; terminal g 3%.\nRed-team concerns: valuation embeds $5B FY2030 revenue; NAND normalization 2027;\nWC drain; GAAP/adjusted gap unexplained; crowding 10/15. If my FY27 growth (>16%)\nis wrong, base IV falls toward $60-90. If FY27 guide validates >20% growth and\nMonTitan mix >10%, re-run the model immediately.\n\n\u25a0 LAYER 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\nACTION: AVOID \u2014 DO NOT INITIATE.\nConviction: MEDIUM-HIGH on the conclusion (negative MoS), not on the long case.\nWeight for new money: 0% (Kelly: negative edge; MoS: -137%; base-rate check fails\nat market price).\nIf held: TRIM to \u22642% of portfolio; monitor using Layer 8 KPIs; exit entirely if\nFY27 guide < $1.9B or GM < 46%.\nRe-entry conditions: Price $95-$140, or (FY27 guide \u2265 $2.3B AND MonTitan \u226510% of\nrevenue AND positive FCF with conversion \u226570%), or a 20%+ drawdown to ~$205 with\nconfirmed MonTitan share-gain data and unchanged FY27 guide.\nSummary: A genuinely transformed company at a genuinely demanding price. At $258\nthe market is paying a 9-10% discount-rate and ~$4-5B FY2030 revenue before any\nvalidation. My probability-weighted value is $109; even my bull case ($165) is 36%\nbelow the market price. The asymmetry is unfavorable. Numbers before narrative \u2014\nthe narrative is excellent, and the price already knows.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nEND OF REPORT \u2014 All forecast drivers sourced; items not independently verifiable\nare tagged [Unconfirmed] and carried in the scenario range, not buried.\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```"}, {"sample": 2, "iv": 208.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 459, "report": "```text\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSIMO \u2014 SILICON MOTION TECHNOLOGY CORPORATION \u2014 RS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED ANALYSIS\nT0: 2026-08-24 | ADS Price: $258.36 | Market Cap: $8.671B | 33.56M ADS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\nLayer 0-1. Archetype: D \u2014 PRODUCT-PLATFORM HYBRID, transitioning toward B (Quality\nCompounder). Rationale (3+ reasons): (1) SIMO sells hardware controllers + firmware /\ndesign-win recurring royalties, with enterprise (MonTitan) and automotive (Ferri) creating\ninstalled-base monetization; (2) historical revenue is deeply cyclical (FY23 -32% YoY) \u2014\nthe C (Cyclical) label fit FY2014-FY2024, but FY2025-26 shows a mix shift (Ferri from 4% to\n30% of revenue in 12 months; management claiming \"visibility will improve as company becomes\nless subject to consumer cyclicality\" [Q2'26 call, Actual]); (3) monetization is still\nlargely hardware-design-win-driven, not yet true recurring software/platform revenue, so\nthe full B (Quality Compounder) label is premature.\n\nLayer 0-2. Engine selection: BLENDED \u2014 Engine 3 (Hybrid: Core + Platform) \u00d7 60% + Engine 2\n(Cycle-Adjusted) \u00d7 40%. Transition criteria met: ~50-60% (enterprise/auto ~30-40% of\nrevenue; ROIC > WACC for only the first year, FY26E; GM still in the 48-52% band vs a\ntrue compounder's 60%+). Explicitly NOT: Engine 1 (pure DCF would underweight cyclical\ndownside), Engine 4 (company has positive FCF, not an option-priced pre-revenue story).\n\nLayer 0-3. Transition triggers monitored: (a) Core segment revenue >50% of total and GM\n>30% \u2014 MET (Ferri+MonTitan+enterprise \u2248 30%+, total GM 50%); (b) FCF+ for 2 consecutive\nquarters \u2014 MET (Q1'26/Q2'26 positive); (c) ROIC > WACC for 3 consecutive years \u2014 NOT MET\n(1st year only). Transition condition: 50-80% met \u2192 blended engine applied.\n\nLayer 0-4. Reason for engine choice: Engine 2 alone would capitalize only mid-cycle EPS on\na normal multiple and would miss the structural re-rating toward enterprise/auto visibility;\nEngine 3 alone would ignore the 2023-style cyclical crash risk embedded in any NAND-linked\nname. Errors from alternative choices: Engine 4 would have overvalued a company that still\nneeds to prove >30% OpM durability; Engine 1 would have over-discounted a uniquely\npositioned outsourced-controller merchant in an AI-driven storage super-cycle.\n\nShare-accounting note: Pack wtd-avg shares FY25 = 134.7M ordinary; vendor shares\noutstanding = 33.56M. Reconciliation: 1 ADS = 4 ordinary shares (Macrotrends shows 0.034B\nshares outstanding for 2025, consistent). All per-share metrics below are per ADS.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\nStep 1-1. Macro variables [Actual unless noted]:\n\u2022 10Y UST: 4.65% (YCharts, 2026-08-24) [Actual]; up from 4.30% one year ago.\n\u2022 Real rates: ~2.0-2.2% (est. from 10Y nominal 4.65% minus ~2.5% breakeven) [Estimate].\n\u2022 Fed funds: 3.50-3.75%, unchanged 5 consecutive FOMC meetings; market prices ~1/3\n  probability of a hike (TradingEconomics, 2026-08-24) [Actual].\n\u2022 DXY: ~99.0, weakening over past month (TradingEconomics 2026-08-25) [Actual].\n\u2022 CPI: not directly sourced [Unconfirmed]; Fed holding + hike risk implies sticky ~3%.\n\u2022 Credit spreads: not sourced [Unconfirmed]; net-cash issuer, low relevance.\n\u2022 Economic cycle: Late expansion with bifurcation \u2014 AI/HBM/NAND scarcity boom; consumer\n  weakness (SIMO management: smartphone units -10-15% in 2026) [Actual].\n\u2022 Global liquidity: two-speed; AI-related capex abundant, traditional consumer soft.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime probability distribution:\n\u2022 Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 20%\n\u2022 Reflation (high rates + growth): 45%\n\u2022 Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 20%\n\u2022 Recession (low rates + low growth): 15%\nRationale: high rates (4.65% 10Y) make Goldilocks unlikely; growth is concentrated in AI/\nmemory/semis; base case is Reflation. All scenarios sum to 100%.\n\nStep 1-3. Macro sensitivity matrix (each -3 to +3):\n\u2022 Interest-rate: +1 (net cash $200M; but high multiple is duration sensitive; AI capex\n  financed by big-tech balance sheets, less rate-sensitive)\n\u2022 Dollar: +2 (weak DXY helps Taiwan-based cost competitiveness vs Korean/Japanese rivals;\n  revenue USD-denominated but opex partly TWD)\n\u2022 Liquidity: +1 (AI storage demand funded by hyperscaler capex, not cyclical credit)\n\u2022 Economic-cycle: -1 (consumer eMMC/UFS remains cyclical; offset by enterprise/auto mix)\n\u2022 Commodity/NAND: 0 (NAND scarcity is a double-edged sword: pricing power up, unit\n  volumes capped)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: Regime-weighted impact \u2248 0.20\u00d70 + 0.45\u00d7(+1) + 0.20\u00d70 +\n0.15\u00d7(-1) = +0.30 \u2192 MILD TAILWIND. However 10Y at 4.65% is a genuine headwind for the\nmultiple. Net judgment: NEUTRAL (no conviction adjustment).\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base-rate inquiry:\n\u2022 Probability a cyclical semiconductor company sustains >20% revenue CAGR for 3+ years\n  from a cyclical upcycle starting point: \u2248 25-35% (historical NAND/controller cycles\n  averaged 18-24 months, e.g., 2017-18 peak \u2192 2019 trough; 2021 peak \u2192 2023 trough) [Estimate].\n\u2022 Probability a consumer-component company successfully transitions to enterprise/\n  automotive/AI platform: \u2248 20-30% (industry base rate for platform transition success) [Estimate].\n\u2022 Probability of 5-year survival for a fabless niche semiconductor leader with net cash\n  and NAND-maker relationships: \u2248 80-90% [Estimate].\n\u2022 Probability large competitors (Marvell, Phison) cede enterprise share without a price\n  war: \u2248 30-40% [Estimate].\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate: My posterior success probability (bull+base)\nis ~70% \u2014 significantly above the 25-35% base rate. Justification (3+ company-specific\nfacts): (1) REVENUE REALIZATION: Q1'26 $342M (+? YoY), Q2'26 $451M +127% YoY \u2014 already\ndelivered, not forecast [Actual]; (2) LOCKED DESIGN WINS: MonTitan has 2 Tier-1 customers\nin commercial production, 5 more expected to ramp in H2'26 \u2014 contract-funded, not\nnarrative [Actual, management]; (3) STRUCTURAL SUPPLY SHIFT: NAND makers exiting\nautomotive/consumer controller markets to focus on HBM/DRAM; SIMO is the default merchant\noutsource partner (Ferri 4% \u2192 30% of revenue in one year) [Actual]; (4) R&D PIPELINE:\nPCIe Gen6 tape-out Aug-2026 for hyperscaler 2028 designs [Actual, management]. These\nsupport a higher posterior than base rate \u2014 evidence-based, not ambition-based.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: Success probability 70% > 2.0x base-rate (30%) for\nplatform transition \u2192 RED FLAG. Mandatory re-evaluation performed (see Section 4, 9).\nResolution: success prob trimmed to 70% from 75%; conviction reduced to 8/15 by the\nRed-Team (Section 9). The remaining gap is justified by the 4 points above.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\nStep 2-1. What they do (3 sentences): SIMO designs NAND flash controller ICs and\nintegrated SSD modules \u2014 the \"brains\" inside SSDs, embedded storage (eMMC/UFS), automotive\ndrives (Ferri), and enterprise/AI drives (MonTitan). It sells to NAND makers (Samsung, SK\nHynix, Micron, Kioxia/WDC), module makers, and system OEMs, monetizing through design wins,\nlicense/royalty, and unit shipments. Understandability: MEDIUM-HIGH (semiconductor\nfabless model is straightforward; the technology roadmap and competitive dynamics require\nsemi-industry literacy).\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (7 types, 0-3 each):\n\u2022 Intangible assets (IP, patents): 2 \u2014 strong controller IP portfolio\n\u2022 Switching costs: 2 \u2014 multi-year qualification cycles, firmware lock-in\n\u2022 Network effects: 1 \u2014 NAND-maker interdependence creates quasi-ecosystem lock-in\n\u2022 Cost advantage: 2 \u2014 fabless model; TSMC scale\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2 \u2014 R&D amortization across high volumes\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers: 1 \u2014 automotive/enterprise certifications\n\u2022 Data moat: 1 \u2014 firmware tuning data across installed base\nTOTAL: 11/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT (borderline).\nMoat direction: WIDENING \u2014 evidence: Ferri share gains (4%\u219230%), MonTitan customer ramp,\nNAND makers exiting competitive segments, enterprise design-win pipeline expanding.\nNarrowing triggers monitored: Marvell price cuts, NAND-maker vertical re-integration\npost-2028, PCIe-Gen6 competitive parity.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: FAST GROWER (transitioning out of CYCLICAL). Checkpoints:\n(1) Growth sustainability >20% for 2 more years \u2014 in progress (FY26 +109%, FY27E +11%);\n(2) Balance-sheet strength sufficient to fund growth \u2014 MET (net cash ~$200M);\n(3) Institutional sponsorship \u2014 MET (75% institutional ownership). Risk: categorization\ndepends on FY26-28 execution.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard (FY2025 [Actual] unless noted):\n\u2022 Revenue growth (3-yr CAGR FY22-FY25): -2.2% (cyclical trough); last-2-yr: +26%, +10%\n\u2022 Operating margin (latest, 12-yr median): 10.5% vs 19.0% median \u2192 in recovery\n\u2022 ROE: 14.8% (FY25 NI $123M / equity $831M) [Arithmetic]; vendor ROE 31.6% likely 1-yr-forward [Aggregator]\n\u2022 ROIC (GAAP): ~17% [Estimate], above WACC for the first time in 3 years\n\u2022 FCF margin: +0.7% GAAP; owner-earnings basis: -2.3% (SBC-adjusted) [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: net cash ~$200M, LT debt $3M [Actual]\n\u2022 Interest coverage: N/A (minimal debt)\nJudgment: GOOD (balance-sheet excellent; profitability recovering from trough; FCF weak\nbut inflecting).\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard:\n1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 net issuance -0.1%/yr [Actual]; conservative but no\n   opportunistic buyback.\n2. M&A track record: 2/3 \u2014 historically disciplined; no major impairments in pack.\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 tape-out intensity high, but MonTitan/PCIe-Gen6 are\n   revenue-generating; incremental ROI to be proven.\n4. Mix rationality: 3/3 \u2014 R&D allocated to enterprise/auto/AI storage is the correct\n   strategic bet.\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 Q1'26 guide $292-306M vs actual $342M (beat); Q2'26 guide\n   (implied ~$403M) vs actual $451M (beat); FY26 guide >2x revenue [Actual].\nInsider bonus: 0 (no insider transaction data [Unconfirmed]) \u2192 Capital allocation total:\n12/15 = GOOD-TO-EXCELLENT.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization (3-yr amortization):\n\u2022 FY2030-FY2030 R&D estimate: FY23 \u2248 $200M, FY24 \u2248 $247M, FY25 \u2248 $298M [Estimate \u2014\n  derived as OpEx $335M less SG&A $37M from pack] \u2014 tagged [Estimate] because the pack does\n  not break out R&D explicitly.\n\u2022 Adjusted FY25 OpInc = Reported $93M + R&D $298M - amortization of prior R&D\n  (\u2248 (200+247+298)/3 = $248M) \u2248 $143M [Estimate].\n\u2022 Adjusted NOPAT \u2248 $143M \u00d7 (1-0.22 tax) \u2248 $112M [Estimate].\n\u2022 Unamortized R&D \u2248 $530M [Estimate]; Adjusted Invested Capital = equity $831M + $530M \u2248\n  $1,361M.\n\u2022 Adjusted ROIC (FY25) = 8.2% \u2014 still below WACC (trough year).\n\u2022 FY26E: Adjusted NOPAT \u2248 (OpInc $429M + R&D $330M - amortization ~$292M) \u00d7 0.78 \u2248 $364M;\n  Adjusted IC \u2248 $1,511M; Adjusted ROIC \u2248 24.1% [Estimate \u2014 but directionally meaningful:\n  the GAAP trough legitimately understates returns in an R&D investment year].\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC treatment: FY25 SBC $26M [Actual]; adjusted FCF (OCF $61M - capex $55M -\nSBC $26M) = -$20M [Arithmetic] \u2192 the pack's \"FCF $6M\" overstates by the SBC add-back. In\nthe DCF, GAAP NI already expensed SBC, so no double-count: that FCF is the true equity\nFCF.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization: $14M [Actual FY25] \u2014 immaterial; adjusted net\ndebt stays deeply net cash.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data assets: For a B2B fabless controller company, brand value is\nsubsumed in customer qualifications; no separate mark. Data moat value captured in 1/21\nmoat score; not separately capitalized.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC vs GAAP ROIC: FY25 GAAP ROIC \u2248 17% (because NI includes $65M\nother income while invested capital excludes R&D). Adjusted ROIC 8.2% \u2014 9pp lower \u2014\nexplained by expensed R&D being reclassified as investment. By FY26E the gap narrows:\nGAAP ~24%, adjusted ~24% (converges as R&D growth normalizes).\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett):\nFY25 = NI $123M + D&A $30M - maintenance capex (est. $30M) - \u0394WC $8M = $115M [Estimate].\nFY26E = NI $319M + D&A $38M - maintenance capex (est. $45M) - \u0394WC $77M = $235M [Estimate].\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 BLENDED ENGINE (60% Engine 3 + 40% Engine 2)\n\n3-A. Explicit 5-year forecast (FY2026-FY2030, all figures $B unless noted):\n\n| Metric | FY2025A | FY2026E | FY2027E | FY2028E | FY2029E | FY2030E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 0.886 | 1.850 | 2.050 | 2.350 | 2.650 | 2.900 |\n| Growth | +10.2% | +108.8% | +10.8% | +14.6% | +12.8% | +9.4% |\n| Gross margin | 48.3% | 50.0% | 50.5% | 51.0% | 51.5% | 52.0% |\n| Gross profit | 0.428 | 0.925 | 1.035 | 1.199 | 1.365 | 1.508 |\n| OpEx | 0.335 | 0.496 | 0.605 | 0.671 | 0.730 | 0.790 |\n| OpInc | 0.093 | 0.429 | 0.430 | 0.528 | 0.635 | 0.718 |\n| OpM (GAAP) | 10.5% | 23.2% | 21.0% | 22.5% | 24.0% | 24.8% |\n| Other income | 0.065 | 0.010 | 0.010 | 0.012 | 0.013 | 0.014 |\n| Pre-tax | 0.158 | 0.439 | 0.440 | 0.540 | 0.648 | 0.732 |\n| Tax@22% | 0.035 | 0.097 | 0.097 | 0.119 | 0.143 | 0.161 |\n| Net income | 0.123 | 0.342 | 0.343 | 0.421 | 0.505 | 0.571 |\n| EPS / ADS diluted | $3.66 | $9.20 | $10.22 | $11.70 | $13.84 | $15.44 |\n| D&A | 0.030 | 0.038 | 0.048 | 0.058 | 0.068 | 0.078 |\n| Capex | 0.055 | 0.093 | 0.103 | 0.118 | 0.133 | 0.145 |\n| Capex % rev | 6.2% | 5.0% | 5.0% | 5.0% | 5.0% | 5.0% |\n| \u0394WC (cash drain) | +0.008 | 0.077 | 0.016 | 0.015 | 0.015 | 0.013 |\n| FCF | 0.006 | 0.188 | 0.272 | 0.346 | 0.425 | 0.492 |\n| Diluted ADS | 33.6M | 33.6M | 33.9M | 36.7M | 36.9M | 37.0M |\n\nFORECAST DRIVER SOURCING:\n\u2022 FY26 revenue $1.85B: [Estimate] \u2014 management guides \"more than double YoY\" (\u2265$1.772B);\n  Q1 $342.1M [Actual], Q2 $451M [Actual], Q3 guide $519-541M [Actual], Q4E $527M\n  [Assumption \u2014 flat QoQ; conservative given smartphone-seasonality/NAND-supply offsets].\n\u2022 FY27-30 revenue growth: [Assumption] \u2014 see scenarios; consensus for 2027 is ambiguous\n  (one source states EPS $7.88 [Unconfirmed], we model $10.22 \u2014 above that, justified by\n  MonTitan ramp and company-targeted >30% OpM exit rate); BofA post-Q2 target price\n  of $450 implies >$2B 2028 revenue [Estimate]; we model $2.35B for 2028.\n\u2022 Gross margin: 50-51% from Q3 guide [Actual]; long-term upward drift to 52% [Assumption \u2014\n  enterprise/auto mix should support it; competitive response could cap it (see Section 3.5)].\n\u2022 OpEx: FY26 $496M [Estimate \u2014 Q2 actual $122.1M/quarter; Q3 SBC guide $14.9-15.9M implies\n  OpEx rising; FY25 was $335M, growth to ~$496M] \u2014 FY27-30 OpEx [Assumption].\n\u2022 Tax 22%: [Actual \u2014 management guidance for Q3 and FY26].\n\u2022 Other income: FY25 $65M is unusual (likely FX/investment gains) [Actual];\n  forward $10-14M [Assumption \u2014 interest on cash].\n\u2022 Capex 5.0% of revenue: [Assumption \u2014 pack FY23-FY25 average \u2248 6.5%; we temper to 5% as\n  tape-out costs normalize; upside risk if PCIe-Gen6/Gen7 requires more tooling].\n\u2022 \u0394WC: 8% of incremental revenue FY26, declining to 5% thereafter [Assumption \u2014 historically\n  the working capital swings are large; Q2'26 cash fell $29M due to inventory builds\n  (transcript, Actual)]. Pack [Arithmetic] WC changes are imperfect (they capture only\n  current-assets-minus-liabilities differences and miss some non-cash items). Standard\n  cash-flow WC includes inventory/receivables/payables; for a company doubling revenue,\n  8% of incremental revenue is a reasonable placeholder.\n\u2022 Diluted ADS: 33.6M for FY26 [Actual \u2014 33.56M]; +0.34M SBC dilution FY27 [Assumption];\n  +2.3M convertible-ADS FY28 [Assumption \u2014 $800M convertible notes announced Aug-10-2026,\n  terms [Unconfirmed]; we assume conversion at ~$348 \u2192 2.3M ADS; if the deal does not\n  close, base IV rises ~$12/ADS].\n\u2022 SBC is already expensed in GAAP NI, so FCF = NI + D&A - Capex - \u0394WC is the equity FCF\n  [Assumption \u2014 no other non-cash adjustments beyond D&A material]. FY25 reconciliation\n  suggests this is imperfect (OCF $61M vs NI+D&A-\u0394WC \u2248 $145M), but the direction\n  (conservative vs actual OCF) is acceptable.\n\n3-B. Discount-rate derivation:\n\u2022 Risk-free (10Y UST): 4.65% [Actual \u2014 YCharts 2026-08-24]\n\u2022 ERP: 4.5% [Assumption \u2014 long-run US equity premium]\n\u2022 Beta: 1.15 [Assumption] \u2014 vendor beta 1.714 [Aggregator, window/benchmark unknown]\n  is rejected as backwards-looking on the old consumer-heavy mix; forward beta reflects\n  increasing enterprise/auto/AI revenue (30%+) and net cash. Sensitivity: at beta 1.4,\n  Ke = 10.95%, WACC \u2248 10.2% \u2192 IV drops ~25%; at beta 0.95, Ke = 8.93%, WACC \u2248 8.35% \u2192\n  IV rises ~15%.\n\u2022 Ke = 4.65% + 1.15 \u00d7 4.5% = 9.83% [Assumption]\n\u2022 After-tax cost of debt: 1.0% [Assumption \u2014 $800M converts likely 0-2% coupon; 22% tax\n  shield]. Weight: 8.9% debt / 91.1% equity (at market).\n\u2022 WACC = 0.911 \u00d7 9.83% + 0.089 \u00d7 1.0% = 9.04% \u2192 rounded to 9.0%.\n\n3-C. DCF valuation (WACC 9.0%, terminal growth 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 \u2264 nominal GDP]):\n\u2022 PV(FY26 FCF $188M) = 188/1.09 = $172.5M\n\u2022 PV(FY27 $272M) = 272/1.09\u00b2 = $229.0M\n\u2022 PV(FY28 $346M) = 346/1.09\u00b3 = $267.2M\n\u2022 PV(FY29 $425M) = 425/1.09\u2074 = $301.1M\n\u2022 PV(FY30 $492M) = 492/1.09\u2075 = $319.7M\n\u2022 TV = 492\u00d71.03/(0.09-0.03) = $9,088M; PV = $5,906M\n\u2022 EV = $7,195M\n\u2022 Net cash: cash $202M (pack, FY25) + short-term investments (post-2019 not disclosed \u2192\n  conservative $0) - LT debt $3M + assumed $800M convert proceeds now closed (cash +\n  $800M, debt + $800M \u2192 net $0) \u2192 NET CASH \u2248 $200M [Assumption]\n\u2022 Equity value = $7,395M\n\u2022 Per ADS (36.7M diluted): $201.5\n\nAt WACC 8.5%, TG 3.5% (the generous end): IV = $225-230/ADS.\nAt WACC 9.5%, TG 2.5% (the strict end): IV = $175-180/ADS.\nBase IV point: $210-225; point estimate $215 [Estimate].\n\n3-D. Cross-check multiples (not valuation alone):\n\u2022 FY26E EPS $9.20 \u00d7 28x justified P/E (for +100% revenue growth, PEG = 0.28) = $257.6\n\u2022 FY26E EPS $9.20 \u00d7 24x (normalized-tech P/E) = $220.8\n\u2022 FY27E EPS $10.22 \u00d7 22x (mid-cycle quality) = $224.8\n\u2022 P/E on trailing GAAP (vendor): 31.45x \u2192 overvalued on trailing; fair on forward.\nCross-check median \u2248 $225 \u2192 consistent with DCF IV $215. The market is paying ~$258,\nroughly 15-20% above my fair-value median.\n\n3-E. Reverse DCF: To justify $258 (market cap $8.67B), with WACC 9% and TG 3%, the\nmarket implies steady-state FCF \u2248 $8.67B \u00d7 6% \u2248 $520M by FY28-29. My base case reaches\n$425M (FY29); my bull case reaches $600M+. Thus the market is pricing a ~70% probability\nof the bull trajectory \u2014 optimistic.\n\n3-X. Tornado (top-3 variables by \u00b11\u03c3 impact on IV):\n\u2022 WACC (8.5% vs 9.5%): \u00b1$25\n\u2022 Terminal growth (3.5% vs 2.5%): \u00b1$20\n\u2022 FY27-28 revenue (\u00b110%): \u00b1$15\nThesis KPIs: Q4-26 revenue exit; MonTitan % of Q4 revenue (target 5-10%); GAAP OpM exit;\nOCF conversion; convert-notes closure terms.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response: Marvell (est. ~50% share in enterprise controllers) and\nPhison will respond to MonTitan ramps with aggressive pricing and feature acceleration.\nI embed this as GM capping at 52% and an OpEx floor (marketing/engineering). If SIMO's GM\nholds >52% exiting 2027, expect a price war signal within 12 months. NAND makers\nre-entering automotive post-2028 is a structural risk to Ferri's 30%-of-revenue position.\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity: Positive \u2014 the 2026 stock re-rating ($71 \u2192 $355) enables SBC-based\ntalent attraction (largely expensed at fair value, hence SBC rising to $15M/quarter),\nADS-based M&A currency, and customer trust for enterprise qualification. Negative \u2014 a 50%\ndrawdown (2023-style) would reverse all three.\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer evolution: Early adopters (AI hyperscalers) already in production;\nmainstream enterprise (Tier-2 CSPs, enterprise OEMs) is the 2027-28 marginal buyer; late\nadopters (traditional enterprise storage) will encounter price-based competition from\nMarvell. LTV/CAC: qualification cycles of 12-18 months create long payback but high\nswitching costs \u2014 a net positive for retention once the installed base matures.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\nPrior (from base rates): P(successful platform transition) = 30%.\nLikelihood update from evidence: Q1-Q2'26 actuals (beat + raised), MonTitan production\nramp, Ferri mix shift, NAND scarcity through 2028, management's explicit >2x revenue and\n>30% OpM targets \u2192 posterior:\n\u2022 BEAR: 30%\n\u2022 BASE: 40%\n\u2022 BULL: 30%\n(Success = base + bull = 70% \u2014 still above the 2.0x red flag; Section 9 contains the\nmandatory re-evaluation.)\n\nBear scenario (30%) \u2014 NAND up-cycle fades; MonTitan share gains disappoint; Marvell price\nwar; consumer recession; FY28 revenue $1.65B, EPS $5.50/ADS; 3-yr price target $105\n(18x EPS + $6 div) [Estimate].\n\nBase scenario (40%) \u2014 FY28 revenue $2.35B, EPS $11.70/ADS; 3-yr price target $263\n(22x EPS + $6 div), my DCF-implied. [Estimate].\n\nBull scenario (30%) \u2014 Enterprise/AI/auto mix shifts faster; MonTitan reaches 15-20% of\nrevenue; GM >52%; FY28 revenue $2.80B, EPS $16.50/ADS; 3-yr price $468 (28x + $6 div),\nconsistent with BofA $450 target. [Estimate].\n\nProbability-weighted IV today (discount 3-yr at 10%):\n0.30\u00d7(105/1.331) + 0.40\u00d7(263/1.331) + 0.30\u00d7(468/1.331) =\n0.30\u00d7$78.9 + 0.40\u00d7$197.6 + 0.30\u00d7$351.6 = $23.7 + $79.0 + $105.5 = $208 [Estimate].\n\nMargin of safety:\n\u2022 MoS vs DCF IV $215: (215-258.36)/215 = -20.2% \u2192 NONE.\n\u2022 MoS vs probability-weighted IV $208: (208-258.36)/208 = -24.2% \u2192 NONE.\n\u2022 Even at the generous end ($230): MoS = (230-258)/230 = -12.2% \u2192 NONE / LIMITED.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\nMarket-implied horizon: At $258, the market is capitalizing ~$520M of steady-state FCF\n(\u2248FY28-29 base/bull crossover). Expected 3-yr price from my scenarios $277 \u2192 my horizon\n(3-5 yrs) is aligned with the market's. No horizon arbitrage exists. The market is neither\nshort-sighted nor excessively long; it is pricing a 2-3 year out-year FCF at a full\nmultiple.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\nStep 5-1. Conviction score (5 items, 0-3 each):\n1. Business quality: 2/3 (wide moat borderline; execution strong; history cyclical)\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 1/3 (IV $208-230 vs price $258 \u2192 ~13-24% overvalued)\n3. Macro alignment: 2/3 (AI/semis supercycle; NAND scarcity; rates a drag)\n4. Thesis clarity/KPIs: 3/3 (explicit: >2x FY26 revenue; >30% OpM exit; MonTitan\n   5-10% of Q4 revenue; 2+5 Tier-1 customers)\n5. Execution traceability: 2/3 (recent beats strong; multi-year track record cyclical)\nTOTAL: 10/15 \u2192 HIGH, but after Red-Team downgrade (logic-combat gap <2): 8/15 \u2192 MEDIUM.\n\nStep 5-2. Conviction decay: -0 (no 6-month mark since Q2'26 print; decay starts\nJan-2027).\n\nStep 5-3. Sizing: Medium conviction \u2192 base cap 4-6%. With MoS negative \u2192 no new position\nat T0. Accumulation plan: 50% at \u2264$210, 40% at \u2264$195, 10% at \u2264$180. Max position 5%.\nIf Q4-26 guide >$570M and GAAP OpM >25%, re-score conviction to 10-11/15 and raise\nmax to 7%.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n\u2022 Short interest: 2.75% of float; shares short 807K; days to cover 0.89; MoM change\n  +3.4% [Aggregator \u2014 actual] \u2192 low; no squeeze.\n\u2022 Institutional ownership: 74.99%; insider 3.37% [Aggregator \u2014 13F lag; Actual as reported].\n\u2022 Insider activity: no net buy or sell cluster [Unconfirmed \u2014 no data].\n\u2022 Options positioning: no put/call or skew data [Unconfirmed].\n\u2022 Sentiment extremes: stock +34% on Q2 earnings; sell-side mean target $349, high $450\n  \u2192 bullish tilt, but not a mania. 52-wk range $71.35-$355.00; current price at the 73rd\n  percentile \u2014 the easy money has been made.\n\u2022 Crowding risk score: 4/15 (low short interest, normal institutional, no retail mania;\n  but semis as a sector are a consensus long \u2192 +2 \u2192 6/15). Position-size deduction: -1pp.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\nIn a diversified equity portfolio: SIMO adds Taiwan/Asia semiconductor cyclicality, NAND\nexposure, AI-storage optionality. Correlations: high with SOX (beta ~1.3-1.5 in a\nselloff) and specifically with NAND/memory names (MU, SNDK, WDC). If the book already\nholds NVDA/AMD/MU/AVGO, the marginal diversification benefit is low \u2192 cap at 3%. If the\nbook is underweight semis and has a 3-5 year horizon: size toward 4% on a pullback to\n$210. Dividend yield 0.74% adds a modest income floor. Given the current valuation,\nfade the winner: do not chase strength.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\nUsing 3-yr scenario returns (price only; dividends ignored):\n\u2022 Bear (30%): -52.4% ($258\u2192$123)\n\u2022 Base (40%): +1.8% ($258\u2192$263)\n\u2022 Bull (30%): +64.9% ($258\u2192$426)\nExpected 3-yr return = 0.30\u00d7(-52.4%) + 0.40\u00d7(+1.8%) + 0.30\u00d7(+64.9%) = +4.3% total\n(+1.4%/yr) \u2014 far below cost of equity.\nKelly: win prob 70%, avg win = (0.40\u00d7+1.8 + 0.30\u00d7+64.9)/0.70 = +28.7%, avg loss = -52.4%.\nEdge/odds: Edge = 0.70\u00d728.7 + 0.30\u00d7(-52.4) = +4.3%; Odds = 28.7/52.4 = 0.55.\nKelly = 0.043/0.55 \u2248 7.8%. Half-Kelly = 3.9%; quarter-Kelly \u2248 2%.\nAUDIT CHECK: Layer 5 max (5%) vs Kelly half (3.9%) \u2192 gap < 20pp \u2192 no conflict. Take\nquarter-to-half-Kelly 2-4% IF initiating at T0 \u2014 but MoS is negative, so actual new\nposition at T0 = 0%. If entering at $210 (18% lower), Kelly rises to ~6%; combined with a\nbetter MoS, size 4%.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\nUpside catalysts:\n\u2022 Q3-26 report (late Oct-26) with Q4 guidance >$570M \u2014 would validate acceleration.\n\u2022 MonTitan: 5 additional Tier-1 customers ramping in H2-26; announcement of first\n  PCIe-Gen6 design win (hyperscaler) after Aug-26 tape-out.\n\u2022 NAND scarcity extending past 2028; NAND makers further outsourcing controllers.\n\u2022 Physical-AI/robotics storage opportunity (management: \"larger than automotive\").\n\u2022 Convertible notes closing at a high conversion premium, paired with a buyback.\n\nDownside risks:\n\u2022 NAND scarcity chokes SIMO's own unit volumes as NAND makers prioritize in-house SSDs.\n\u2022 Smartphone units -10-15% in 2026 \u2192 mobile/eMMC/UFS revenue declines despite share gains.\n\u2022 Marvell price war in enterprise controllers; MonTitan share gains stall at small\n  allocations.\n\u2022 $800M convertible closes (or fails); 6-8% dilution overhang \u2014 terms [Unconfirmed].\n\u2022 Working-capital drain: Q1-Q2'26 cash fell ~$29M on inventory builds; if revenue growth\n  pauses, inventory obsolescence hits.\n\u2022 Taiwan geopolitical risk underpriced.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\n\u2022 Common bear triggers shared with the portfolio: (1) US 10Y rising above 5% \u2014 hits all\n  long-duration tech; (2) USD strength (DXY >105) \u2014 hits Taiwan/semis broadly; (3) AI\n  capex digestion pause \u2014 hits SOX complex. SIMO's idiosyncratic tail: 2023-style NAND\n  down-cycle (-32% revenue), which can occur while the index is flat.\n\u2022 Tail correlation (2008/2020/2022 analogs): SIMO beta 1.3-1.5 to SOX in drawdowns \u2192\n  diversification value of this position is LOW; expect 55-70% drawdown in a 2022-style\n  regime (rates + semis) vs 50% for SOX.\n\u2022 CVaR (5% worst 1-yr): -55% (from $258 to ~$116), consistent with the analyst low target\n  $145 plus overshoot.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\nStep 7-1. Thesis-integrity thresholds (violation triggers re-evaluation):\n\u2022 Revenue: Q4-26 guide on the next call must be \u2265$500M (implies FY26 \u2265$1.82B). Below\n  $450M = FLAG.\n\u2022 GM: Q3-26 GM guide 50-51% \u2014 if actual prints <48%, margin thesis damaged.\n\u2022 GAAP OpM exit: Q4-26 non-GAAP OpM \u226530%; GAAP OpM \u226525% required to keep my margin path.\n\u2022 MonTitan: must exit 2026 at 5-10% of revenue as guided; zero or <3% = core thesis broken.\n\u2022 Ferri: must stay \u226520% of revenue; a drop below 15% signals share loss.\n\u2022 OCF conversion: FY26 OCF \u2265$300M (vs NI $342M); if OCF <$200M and inventory keeps\n  building, downgrade FCF model.\n\u2022 Convertible notes: monitor closure terms. If closed, track conversion dilution; if\n  canceled, raise IV by ~$12/ADS.\n\nStep 7-4. Pre-mortem \u2014 \"If SIMO fails by 2031, why?\":\n1. The NAND up-cycle of 2025-26 was a price-inflation mirage; 2027-28 unit recession\n   drops revenue to $1.3-1.5B and MonTitan wins fail to convert to volume. Early\n   indicator: Q4-26/Q1-27 unit data showing controller shipments flat/down despite\n   revenue growth.\n2. Marvell launches a PCIe-Gen6 part with 30% better perf/W and cuts price 20%,\n   freezing SIMO at low single-digit enterprise share. Early indicator: enterprise\n   design-win announcements by Marvell/Phison in 2027; SIMO GM <49% despite mix.\n3. NAND makers internalize controllers after the HBM capex cycle ends (2028+), choking\n   SIMO's supply and customer base. Early indicator: Samsung/SK Hynix/Micron announce\n   in-house enterprise controller investments in 2027-28.\n\nStep 7-5. Decision journal entry: Logged in Section 11.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\nStep 9-1. Short thesis (same data):\n1. \"Structural platform transition\" is a cyclical up-cycle in a NAND shortage; 2017 and\n   2021 both had identical narratives, followed by 2019 (-14% revenue) and 2023\n   (-32%). This time is no different until proven otherwise; the 2026 >2x revenue is\n   price inflation on a supply-constrained unit base.\n2. MonTitan is 3 years late vs Marvell in enterprise; 7 Tier-1 customers sound like a\n   pipeline, not a P&L; enterprise customers allocate strategically and will not risk\n   their AI infrastructure on a Taiwan merchant that NAND makers could cut off.\n3. The $800M convertible (Aug-10-2026) is insider knowledge that the stock is rich \u2014\n   management sells equity at $340+ conversion before proving 2027 revenues.\n4. Ferri at 30% of revenue is a concentration in \"boot drives\" \u2014 a niche, low-barrier,\n   low-ASP segment that Samsung could crush if it chose; AI-server boot storage is\n   not a long-term moat.\n5. OCF quality is poor: FY25 OCF $61M vs NI $123M (49% conversion); H1-26 cash fell;\n   the company is pre-funding inventory at the top of the cycle.\n6. Gross margin 50% is an outlier vs the 12-yr median (48% and falling); Marvell's\n   enterprise cost base will compete it back to 45% within 24 months.\n7. Taiwan cross-strait risk: an escalation shadows every ADR; no ex-Taiwan design/manu\n   diversification.\n\nStep 9-2. Logic combat: Long 6.5/10 vs Short 5.5/10 \u2192 gap = 1.0 < 2 \u2192 CONVICTION\nDOWNGRADED from 10 to 8 (High\u2192Medium). The short case is credible and data-grounded,\neven if the base case remains likely.\n\nStep 9-3. Adversarial data: No Hindenburg-type short report exists [Unconfirmed].\nThe bear case rests on cycle history and Marvell's position rather than fraud/accounting\nallegations. The pack's Beneish M-score was deliberately omitted; Piotroski F = 5/8\n(adequate, not robust); Accruals ratio 0.0544 warns that FY25 earnings are not yet cash.\n\nStep 9-4. Killed arguments (and resurrection conditions):\n\u2022 \"NAND makers will never re-enter automotive/controllers \u2014 it's unprofitable for them.\"\n  KILLED by: their public exits and SIMO's Ferri 30% share. REVIVED IF: SIMO GM >52%\n  persists into 2028 and NAND maker HBM capex intensity fades \u2014 then internalization\n  economics flip.\n\u2022 \"High beta 1.714 means the stock is risky.\" KILLED by: the vendor beta [Unconfirmed\n  window/benchmark] and mix shift to enterprise/auto. REVIVED IF: consumer revenue\n  share returns to >60% of total.\n\u2022 \"The 2026 growth is unsustainable.\" KILLED by: Q1/Q2 actuals + Q3 guide + NAND\n  scarcity through 2028. REVIVED IF: Q4-26 guide <$450M.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\nA. Methodology audit: Blended Engine 3/2 is appropriate for a transition-stage cyclical-\nplatform hybrid. No DCF-only conclusion; multiples and reverse-DCF used. PASS.\nB. Valuation audit: Base IV $208-230 (probability-weighted $208; DCF $201.5 at 9% WACC /\n  3% TG; generous $225-230 at 8.5% / 3.5%). Cross-check median $225. Range consistent.\n  FAIL \u2014 the WACC beta input (1.15 [Assumption]) drives \u00b125% of IV; flagged in Section 3-B.\nC. Bias audit: Potential anchoring to sell-side targets (mean $349) rejected in favor of\n  DCF. Potential confirmation bias toward management guidance; mitigated by the Red Team\n  section. PASS/CAUTION.\nD. Consistency audit: Per-ADS basis used throughout; GAAP FCF used in DCF; SBC not\n  double-counted; scenarios sum to 100%. PASS.\nE. Narrative audit: The AI supercycle narrative is supported by actual Q1/Q2 revenue and\n  customer counts, not TAM estimates. PASS.\nF. Red-team audit: Logic gap 1.0 \u2192 conviction reduced 10 \u2192 8. PASS.\nG. Data integrity audit: Pack Tier 1/2 data solid (FY14-25 filings). Q1-26/Q2-26 data\n  from transcripts/company releases [Actual]. Convertible terms [Unconfirmed]. FY27\n  consensus [Unconfirmed \u2014 conflicting vendor figures]. Existing-pile R&D breakdown\n  [Estimate]. Model Confidence: MEDIUM-LOW \u2014 not because of the base case, but because\n  the IV range spans $180-230 across reasonable WACC/TG inputs.\n\n37-point check: No single material failure requiring full recalculation; but the\n[$800M convert terms] and [2027 consensus] gaps are explicitly carried into the\nscenario range. The fair value is robustly below $258 across all reasonable inputs\nexcept beta <0.95 or TG >4%, both outside my credible range.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\nDate: 2026-08-24 | T0 ADS price: $258.36 | Market cap: $8.671B | IV: $208-230,\npoint $215 | MoS: -15% to -25% (negative).\nKey assumptions: FY26 revenue $1.85B, GM 50%, OpM 23%; FY27 revenue $2.05B, EPS\n$10.22; WACC 9%; TG 3%; convert closes, +2.3M ADS by FY28; scenario probabilities\n30/40/30.\nRed-team concerns: (1) NAND cycle peak risk; (2) MonTitan pipeline \u2260 revenue;\n(3) convert-dilution overhang; (4) OCF quality.\nRe-evaluation triggers: Q3-26 report (late Oct-26): Q4 guide <$450M \u2192 thesis broken,\nfair value $140-160; Q4-26 guide >$570M \u2192 fair value $250-260, upgrade to BUY.\nDecision: HOLD / DO NOT INITIATE.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\nACTION: HOLD \u2014 DO NOT INITIATE NEW POSITIONS AT $258.36. Hold existing positions\n(\u22643% weight), do not add until the accumulation zone.\nCONVICTION: MEDIUM \u2014 8/15 (post-Red-Team downgrade from 10/15).\nWEIGHT: 0% new money at T0. Accumulate: 50% of intended position at \u2264$210; 40% at\n\u2264$195; 10% at \u2264$180. Maximum total position: 4-5% (quarter-Kelly 2-4% + conviction cap).\n\nSTRATEGY:\n1. If held: maintain 2-3%; trail a mental stop at $190 (25% below T0) \u2014 below that,\n   the NAND-cycle bear case dominates and the stock loses its platform multiple.\n2. If not held: place limit orders at $210/$195/$180; do not chase strength; if Q4-26\n   guide comes in >$570M and GAAP OpM >25%, re-price the stock to $250-260 and accept\n   a 5% position on the next pullback.\n3. Monitor (Section 8): quarterly guide-vs-actual; MonTitan %; Ferri %; OCF conversion;\n   convert-notes closure/terms.\n4. Re-score conviction every 6 months; decay -1 if no confirming evidence at each\n   checkpoint (next: Q3-26 report, late Oct-2026).\n\nBottom line: SIMO is a high-quality, wide-moat, fast-growing semiconductor franchise at\na critical inflection. At $258, the market is paying full price for a high-probability\nbull case; there is no margin of safety. The expected 3-year return is ~1-5%/yr at T0,\nvs ~12%/yr if entered at $210. Patience is the arbitrage. Wait for the accumulation zone\nand let the Q3-26 report either de-risk (buy) or confirm the bear case (avoid).\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nEND OF ANALYSIS \u2014 RS2 v2.0